WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.016
This is one frame from the telescope.

00:00:02.016 --> 00:00:04.739
It's short, noisy, and the nebula is barely there.

00:00:04.739 --> 00:00:08.988
Stacking is how we turn hundreds of frames like this into a single deep image.

00:00:08.988 --> 00:00:10.895
OriginStack does it in four phases.

00:00:10.895 --> 00:00:12.202
Let's walk through them.

00:00:12.702 --> 00:00:14.370
Phase one prepares every frame.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:17.653
Each one is loaded and calibrated, then hot pixels are fixed.

00:00:17.653 --> 00:00:20.882
Hot pixels are sensor defects that show up as bright specks.

00:00:20.882 --> 00:00:27.501
The frame is then debayered, which turns the sensor's colour mosaic into a full colour image, and the white balance is set.

00:00:27.501 --> 00:00:32.453
While that happens, the program scores each frame for sharpness, star shape, and background.

00:00:32.453 --> 00:00:38.318
Frames that are blurred by wind, smeared by a passing cloud, or crossed by a satellite trail can be rejected.

00:00:38.318 --> 00:00:40.740
We want only good light going into the stack.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:45.386
The sky moves between frames, and the telescope's tracking is never perfect.

00:00:45.386 --> 00:00:47.022
Phase two lines everything up.

00:00:47.022 --> 00:00:51.877
It first finds a rough shift, then refines it to a fraction of a pixel by matching stars.

00:00:51.877 --> 00:00:54.767
If the field has rotated, it fits a rotation as well.

00:00:54.767 --> 00:00:56.295
It also checks its own work.

00:00:56.295 --> 00:01:02.022
Any frame whose stars don't line up well enough is dropped, rather than being allowed to blur the result.

00:01:02.522 --> 00:01:04.500
Now the aligned frames are combined.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.290
The signal from the target is the same in every frame, so it adds up.

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:10.597
The noise is random, so it partly cancels.

00:01:10.597 --> 00:01:15.926
For each pixel, the program looks at its value across all the frames and throws out the outliers.

00:01:15.926 --> 00:01:19.112
That removes satellites, cosmic rays, and airplane lights.

00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:26.088
By default it also uses a proper coaddition, which weights each frame by how good it is, so sharp, clean frames count for more.

00:01:26.088 --> 00:01:28.505
Same sky, but now the faint detail is there.

00:01:29.005 --> 00:01:32.209
The stack is still linear data, so it looks dark and flat.

00:01:32.209 --> 00:01:33.811
Phase four brings it to life.

00:01:33.811 --> 00:01:37.622
It removes the sky glow gradient, reduces colour noise, and denoises.

00:01:37.622 --> 00:01:41.157
Stars can be tightened, and local contrast brings out structure.

00:01:41.157 --> 00:01:45.023
Finally the image is stretched, which lifts the faint parts into view.

00:01:45.023 --> 00:01:53.142
Every step can be switched off or tuned, and the program saves the untouched linear stack, so you can redo this phase later without stacking again.

00:01:53.642 --> 00:01:57.232
Hundreds of faint, noisy frames became one detailed image.

00:01:57.232 --> 00:01:59.212
Process, align, combine, finish.

00:01:59.212 --> 00:02:00.636
That's the whole stack.

00:02:00.636 --> 00:02:01.874
Thanks for watching.
